You choose freely between -sei- and -sep- (and -sepl- and -sepli-, since
all gismu have themselves and their first four letters as rafsi), but if
your first rafsi ends in a vowel, you need to put a buffer consonant (in
this case "r") after it; the first five letters of a lujvo must contain a
consecutive pair of consonants.

So {seirjo'e} and {sepyjo'e} are valid, but {seijo'e} is not.

On Sun, Jul 8, 2012 at 3:50 PM, Kosmaton <Kosmaton@gmx.com> wrote:

> Thank you for the extra explanations, stevo and remod!
>
> I just had another look around jbovlaste, and perhaps the imbalance
> between the structure of {fendi} and that of {jorne} can be removed by
> substituting {sepli} for {fendi}?
>
> {jorne} : x1 is joined to/connects to/is united with x2 at common
> locus x3; x1 and x2 are a union
> {sepli}: x1 is apart/separate from x2, separated by partition/wall/gap/
> interval/separating medium x3
>
> Those seem balanced opposites to me.
>
> So, {la seijo'e}? At {sepli} the listed rafsi are {sep} and {sei} -- I
> find {sei} sounds better, but can one choose freely? Are there again
> morphological additions to be made?
>
> cheers
